  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is chlorophyll?",
      "a": "Chlorophyll is the green pigment inside the chloroplasts of plant cells. It captures light energy that the plant uses to make sugar in photosynthesis.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes leaves green?",
        "Where is chlorophyll found?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why don't leaves use green light?",
      "a": "Leaves do use some green light, but chlorophyll absorbs it much less than red and blue. So more green bounces back or shines through, and we see the leaf as green.",
      "asks": [
        "Do plants use green light at all?",
        "Why do plants reflect green?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What colours of light do leaves use?",
      "a": "Chlorophyll absorbs mostly blue-violet light and red light. Other pigments, like orange carotenoids, catch some extra blue and green.",
      "asks": [
        "Which light is best for plants?",
        "Do plants like red light?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do leaves turn yellow and red in autumn?",
      "a": "As days shorten, trees stop making chlorophyll and it breaks down. Yellow and orange pigments that were there all along show through, and some trees make new red pigments.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do leaves change colour?",
        "Where do autumn colours come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is white light made of?",
      "a": "White light, like sunlight, is a mixture of all the colours of the rainbow. A leaf takes out mostly red and blue and sends back mostly green.",
      "asks": [
        "Is sunlight made of colours?",
        "What colours are in sunlight?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Chlorophyll absorbs mostly blue and red light and absorbs green light less, so leaves look green.",
    "Autumn yellows and oranges come from carotenoid pigments that are present all summer but hidden by chlorophyll."
